    "interpretation": "A fast orbital receiver traverses ~a full TEP correlation length per gap, so there is no stable spatial relationship between the LEO and a fixed ground network to exploit -- the ground prior does not beat persistence and progressively hurts as the gap (and ground-track distance) grows.  The ground-to-ground control reproduces the small positive Step 55/56 result on the same machinery, so the LEO negative is physical, not a pipeline artefact.  The TEP clock-transfer benefit requires the receiver to be quasi-stationary relative to the field's correlation length over the outage -- satisfied by urban/pedestrian/IoT/maritime/fixed/slow-vehicle receivers and timing holdover, not by a 7.5 km/s orbital platform."
